<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  The Northrop Grumman B-2A Spirit's published figures, and the conditions
  each was measured in, so far as they are given.

  From the Air Force's B-2 Spirit fact sheet (current as of December 2015),
  the only primary source that gives any: its speed is "high subsonic", its
  ceiling 50,000 ft, and its range "approximately 6,000 nautical miles"
  unrefuelled, on no stated profile or payload. The checks in
  src/sim/figures.cpp fly one flight per <figure> and compare what JSBSim does
  with the range given here; the tolerances are this project's.
-->
<!--
  **It is flown down an approach with its drag rudders half open**
  (approach_speedbrake, the speedbrake lever the approach autopilot holds).
  It has no flap; opened together its split drag rudders are its speedbrakes,
  and they are open in flight at low speed - tools/make_b2.py gives the
  sources. How far is not published: 0.5 is calibrated so that the
  approach is flown at its reference speed with about a third of its
  throttle on, room to slow and to speed up, and is not a published figure.
  Shut, it crossed the threshold fourteen knots fast with its throttles
  closed. Only the drag at the setting matters, and the drag is linear in
  the lever: half of it is half the drag the model gives them fully open.
-->
<!--
  **touchdown_fpm** is the sink the AI's flare brings the wheels to the
  runway at, not a published figure. A jet is flown on to the runway rather
  than held off it to float (the FAA's Airplane Flying Handbook,
  FAA-H-8083-3C, chapter 16): two hundred feet a minute, a third of the
  600 the server judges any gear to take (sim/crash.hpp). Held off to the
  forty a light aeroplane is, a B-2A in ground effect floated two
  kilometres down the runway.
-->
<published_figures aircraft="b2" flaps_full_deg="0" approach_speedbrake="0.5" touchdown_fpm="200">
  <source>USAF B-2 Spirit fact sheet</source>

  <!-- 336,500 lb, the maximum take-off weight: 160,000 lb, taken as the empty
       weight; two crew, 460 lb; 167,000 lb of fuel; and the 9,040 lb left, as
       weapons. -->
  <loading name="maximum" total_lbs="336500">
    <pointmass index="0" lbs="460"/>
    <pointmass index="1" lbs="9040"/>
    <tank index="0" lbs="41750"/>
    <tank index="1" lbs="41750"/>
    <tank index="2" lbs="41750"/>
    <tank index="3" lbs="41750"/>
  </loading>

  <!-- 253,000 lb: the same with half its fuel, a weight in the cruise. -->
  <loading name="cruise" total_lbs="253000">
    <pointmass index="0" lbs="460"/>
    <pointmass index="1" lbs="9040"/>
    <tank index="0" lbs="20875"/>
    <tank index="1" lbs="20875"/>
    <tank index="2" lbs="20875"/>
    <tank index="3" lbs="20875"/>
  </loading>

  <!-- The empty weight, its crew, and a tenth of its fuel: as light as it
       flies. -->
  <loading name="light" total_lbs="177160">
    <pointmass index="0" lbs="460"/>
    <pointmass index="1" lbs="0"/>
    <tank index="0" lbs="4175"/>
    <tank index="1" lbs="4175"/>
    <tank index="2" lbs="4175"/>
    <tank index="3" lbs="4175"/>
  </loading>

  <figure name="high_subsonic_speed" fuel_frozen="1" flight="level_mach" loading="cruise"
          unit="Mach" min="0.80" max="0.95" altitude_ft="40000" mach="0.75">
    The fact sheet's speed, "high subsonic": level at 40,000 ft at full
    throttle, between Mach 0.80 and 0.95, as this project reads it.
  </figure>

  <figure name="climb_rate_50000_ft" fuel_frozen="1" flight="max_climb_rate" loading="light"
          unit="ft/min" min="100" max="20000" altitude_ft="50000" from_kcas="180" to_kcas="300">
    The fact sheet's ceiling, 50,000 ft: as light as it flies, at full
    throttle, it still climbs there at 100 ft/min, the rate that makes a
    service ceiling.
  </figure>

  <figure name="range" flight="cruise_range" loading="maximum" unit="nmi" value="6000"
          tolerance_percent="15" altitude_ft="40000" mach="0.8">
    The fact sheet's range, "approximately 6,000 nautical miles" unrefuelled:
    no profile or payload is given; flown level at 40,000 ft and Mach 0.8,
    this project's choice, from its maximum take-off weight to the last of its
    fuel.
  </figure>

  <figure name="stall_speed_landing" flight="stall_speed" from="measured"
          loading="light" unit="KCAS" value="95.42" tolerance="3"
          flaps_deg="0" entry_kcas="200" gear="1">
    **Measured, not published.** No flight manual for this aeroplane is
    public, so there is no handbook stall speed to hold the model to. This is
    what the model itself does, flown by the same deceleration the published
    stall speeds are measured by, on 2026-09-23. What it asserts is that the
    model still does what it did, not that the aeroplane does it. Measured
    from entry speeds of 160, 200 and 240 knots it varies by 0.08 of a
    knot, so the number is the model's and not the entry's.
  </figure>

  <figure name="climb_rate" from="measured" loading="light" unit="ft/min"
          value="8271" tolerance_percent="5" speed_kcas="230">
    **Measured, not published.** No flight manual for this aeroplane is
    public, so there is no handbook rate of climb and no handbook climbing
    speed. Both are the model's own, taken on 2026-09-23. Sweeping 200 to 420 knots the best rate is 8621 ft/min at 340 knots; this is the
    lowest speed that still climbs within ninety-five per cent of it, which is
    the speed a climb is actually flown at. Taking the peak itself would have
    taught a climb faster than the aeroplane is operated.
  </figure>
  <!-- **The speeds a plan may fly her at**, measured by `glideslope_cli
       plan-speeds b2` on 2026-10-07 (linux-release): the tightest orbit a
       plan may ask at each speed (sim/orbit_trial.hpp), flown clean by the
       navigator and autopilot at 3,000 ft, both ways round, in calm air and
       in a 10 kt wind, each to hold its height within 50 ft and its speed
       within 5 kt, and a heading held on the autopilot alone in calm air
       and a 20 kt crosswind (sideslip within a degree, heading within two);
       the slowest sought up from its approach speed in 5 kt steps,
       the fastest down from a fifth over its start speed, and written by
       the command's one rule (src/frontend/cli/main.cpp, plan_speeds). -->
  <plan_speeds slowest_kcas="159" fastest_kcas="300">
    The slowest, 159 kt, is 5 kt over the first that held, 154: her approach
    speed is a flaps-down figure, and flown clean below 154 kt she came down
    500 ft before reaching her circle. At 154 she held the orbit all four
    ways and, on her own slower rudder integral (her catalogue's
    `yaw-damper`), her sideslip in the crosswind within 0.58 degree. On
    2026-10-02, before that integral, she swung it 7.3 degrees either way at
    159 kt and held it only from 189, which was then her slowest less 5. The
    fastest, 300 kt, a fifth over its start speed, held all four ways.
  </plan_speeds>
  <!-- **The slowest a glide may fly it at**, measured by `glideslope_cli
       glide-speeds b2` on 2026-10-07 (linux-release): from the slowest a
       route may fly it up in 5 kt steps, each glided round the tightest
       orbit allowed from 30,000 ft, every engine stopped, clean, both ways
       round (sim::glide_tightest_orbit), until one went round without its
       wing passing the angle its lift peaked at, nor 25 degrees. -->
  <glide_speeds slowest_kcas="159">
    159 kt, the slowest a route may fly it, went round unstalled: at 13.6
    and 13.7 degrees of alpha, where her lift peaked at 13.5 and 13.7.
  </glide_speeds>
</published_figures>
